\details{
Complements \code{\link[=diagnose_drift]{diagnose_drift()}}. Where that tests for a \emph{gradual} monotonic
trend, this tests for a \strong{single abrupt shift} (e.g. a reporting-system change
on some date) in the per-period delay summaries, using \strong{Pettitt's}
nonparametric change-point test. As with \code{\link[=diagnose_drift]{diagnose_drift()}} it works on both
a location statistic (median / mean) and a dispersion statistic (IQR / 10-90
spread), on mature data only, and — being rank-based — it is robust to the
skew and serial dependence of a delay series.
}
\section{Interpreting the result}{


Judge \code{shift} first and \code{p_value} second. A statistically detected change
point with a \code{shift} far smaller than the day-to-day noise in the delay
series is not worth acting on; a large \code{shift} is, even at a marginal
p-value.

Two structural caveats matter in practice:
\itemize{
\item Pettitt's test assumes \strong{exactly one} change point. Given several, it
returns the most prominent and silently ignores the rest. If you suspect
more, re-run on each side of the first \code{changepoint} to search recursively.
\item A slow monotonic drift will often trip this test too, with the change point
landing near the middle of the series. Running \code{\link[=diagnose_drift]{diagnose_drift()}}
alongside disambiguates: a genuine step shows up here and not
necessarily there, while a gradual drift shows up in both.
}

A confirmed change point usually has an operational explanation — a new
laboratory information system, a change in case definition, a reporting
mandate, a holiday backlog being cleared. Where it lands is a strong hint
about the cause, and about how far back a nowcasting model can safely be
fitted: data before the change point comes from a different reporting regime.

Unlike \code{\link[=diagnose_drift]{diagnose_drift()}}, this test has no third-party dependency and no
meaningful runtime cost, so it is cheap to run routinely.
}

